<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="dimensions-overview" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>The Six Dimensions of Culturally Responsive Teaching</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      These six dimensions aim to capture the essence of Culturally Responsive Teaching in our
      informal math enrichment program (and we believe that can be helpful for other programs).
      Each dimension corresponds to a hexagon in the diagram. We also describe the
      <q>fundamental goal</q> of our program (in the center of the diagram), as well as the three
      general contexts in which the six dimensions materialize (the concentric circles).
    </p>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>The six dimensions</title>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>Students at the center</li>
          <li>Relationships and caring</li>
          <li>Deep thinking</li>
          <li>Access and relevance</li>
          <li>Inclusion through cultural diversity</li>
          <li>Social justice reflection</li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>The fundamental goal</title>
      <p>Personal Development &amp; Growth</p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>The three contexts</title>
      <p>Program structure, Pedagogical practices, Curriculum</p>
    </paragraphs>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="dimensions-summary">
    <title>Summary of the Six Dimensions</title>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Dimension 1: Students at the center</title>
      <dl>
        <li><title>1.1 Adaptability</title>
          <p>Design and adjust activities based on students' cultures, interests, and real-time engagement.</p>
        </li>
        <li><title>1.2 Ongoing Guidance</title>
          <p>Anticipate, observe, question, and support students to address needs and advance understanding.</p>
        </li>
        <li><title>1.3 Students' Interactions</title>
          <p>Foster students' collaboration from individual thinking to sharing ideas, helping others, and reasoning together.</p>
        </li>
        <li><title>1.4 Make Connections</title>
          <p>Link student ideas, previous understanding, and different contexts to consolidate the learning goals.</p>
        </li>
      </dl>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Dimension 2: Relationships and caring</title>
      <dl>
        <li><title>2.1 Welcome Students</title>
          <p>Use your words and actions to create a positive, welcoming space where meaningful learning can happen.</p>
        </li>
        <li><title>2.2 Find Ways to Connect</title>
          <p>Actions that encourage meaningful, two-way exchanges with students.</p>
        </li>
        <li><title>2.3 Build confidence and trust</title>
          <p>Help students trust themselves, you, and the learning process.</p>
        </li>
        <li><title>2.4 Be a Role Model</title>
          <p>Show students how to approach challenges, think critically, and behave in a responsible way.</p>
        </li>
      </dl>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Dimension 3: Deep thinking</title>
      <dl>
        <li><title>3.1 Prepare for deep content and relational structures</title>
          <p>Get ready so that activities are geared toward deep reasoning.</p>
        </li>
        <li><title>3.2 Pave the way for deep thinking</title>
          <p>Create a learning environment to encourage students to reason, notice patterns, and connect ideas.</p>
        </li>
        <li><title>3.3 Support students' attempts at reasoning</title>
          <p>Help students clarify their thinking, scaffold their reasoning, and overcome conceptual challenges.</p>
        </li>
        <li><title>3.4 Guide students through challenges</title>
          <p>Encourage persistence, resilience, and productive struggle in problem-solving.</p>
        </li>
      </dl>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Dimension 4: Access and relevance</title>
      <dl>
        <li><title>4.1 Meaningful Tasks</title>
          <p>Include activities that connect to students' cultures and lives, and spark curiosity.</p>
        </li>
        <li><title>4.2 Accessible language</title>
          <p>Explain clearly, and support diverse language and communication.</p>
        </li>
        <li><title>4.3 Powerful representations</title>
          <p>Use manipulatives, visuals, and models effectively to build understanding.</p>
        </li>
        <li><title>4.4 Empower to use modalities flexibly</title>
          <p>Help students choose and adapt ways to represent ideas and systems.</p>
        </li>
      </dl>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Dimension 5: Inclusion through cultural diversity</title>
      <dl>
        <li><title>5.1 Cultural Identities</title>
          <p>Value who students are and where they come from, showing pride in their cultures and making their practices visible.</p>
        </li>
        <li><title>5.2 Content Linked with Culture</title>
          <p>Intentionally connect teaching content to cultural experiences, world scenarios, and diverse perspectives.</p>
        </li>
        <li><title>5.3 Inclusive Practices</title>
          <p>Design learning spaces and activities where all voices, traditions, and cultural ways of learning feel welcome.</p>
        </li>
        <li><title>5.4 Respect &amp; Critical Awareness</title>
          <p>Guide students to explore unfamiliar cultures out of their comfort zone, discuss differences respectfully, and challenge stereotypes humbly, with curiosity and care.</p>
        </li>
      </dl>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Dimension 6: Social justice reflection</title>
      <p>(See the Dimension 6 section for full details.)</p>
    </paragraphs>

  </subsection>

</section>
